  "conclusions": [
    "All three20s fixed native target holds lose the ball despite submillimetre arm reference errors and zero hold-stage clipping/rate limiting. Transport trajectory, accumulated Cartesian error and a change in gripper command are not necessary for this simulated failure.",
    "Episode1 ball creeps along tool X while opposed jaw normals become increasingly non-opposed; both jaws retain substantial compressive normal force immediately before escape. The episode1 direct trace exactly reproduces the prior root-cause replay.",
    "This supports unstable retention/contact creep under the candidate scene. It does not uniquely identify jaw surface shape, gripper encoder mapping/zero, actuator stiffness, friction or contact regularization as the physical root cause.",
    "The candidate scene predicts about12mm larger tip gaps from recorded real opening than from its simulated loaded opening at the matched hold anchors. Tip gap is a geometry proxy, not a measured inner-pad separation.",
    "The unchanged two historical placements are not evidence of long-duration retention: both also drop when held longer at fixed targets."
  ],
  "next_independent_measurements": [
    "Measure unloaded inner-jaw separation and tip/pad landmarks at several ordinary follower encoder openings including empty closure; fit percent-to-hinge mapping/zero without ball contact, success selection or per-episode offsets.",
    "Measure actual ball diameter, rigid jaw/pad surface shape and thickness; compare loaded contact depth and pad orientation in calibrated two views after freezing the unloaded mapping.",
    "Record requested, bounded/sent and measured gripper targets during matched empty/loaded holds; compare actual timing and loaded opening. Existing command provenance is unverified.",
    "Only after independent geometry measurement, compare held-out recorded grasps and the same fixed-hold protocol; do not raise grip force/friction or relax safety constraints to make these development episodes pass."
